  "slug": "artist:charlie-parker",
  "name": "Charlie Parker",
  "editorial_article": "Charles \"Charlie\" Parker Jr. (1920–1955), affectionately nicknamed \"Bird\" or \"Yardbird,\" was an American jazz saxophonist, composer, and a central architect of the bebop revolution. Born in Kansas City, Kansas, Parker emerged from the regional jazz scenes of the late 1930s to become one of the most influential figures in the history of music. His innovations on the alto saxophone—characterized by lightning-fast tempos, intricate melodic lines, and advanced harmonic vocabularies including revolutionary use of chord substitutions—fundamentally reshaped the trajectory of jazz. Moving away from the dance-oriented big band swing era, Parker helped establish jazz as an intellectually rigorous, virtuoso art form.\n\nParker's legacy is defined by his period of intense musical growth, particularly his legendary \"woodshedding\" sessions in the late 1930s, where he reportedly practiced up to 15 hours a day. By the early 1940s, his work in New York City alongside other pioneers like Dizzy Gillespie transformed the sound of jazz, moving it into the small-group \"bebop\" format. Despite a life shortened by severe drug addiction and systemic challenges, Parker’s recordings for labels like Savoy, Dial, and Verve remain essential listening. He stood not only as a titan of technical proficiency but as a cultural icon for the Beat Generation, embodying the image of the uncompromising, introspective modern artist. His influence extends far beyond his instrument, serving as a primary point of reference for countless improvisers and composers across all subsequent jazz styles.",
